*The sums at $p=3$ with $ZZ slash 9$: the reduction.* Let $A=D_(3)(T')$, $B=D_(3)(Y)$, and let $C subset A perp B$ be the isotropic graph of a primitive gluing. Primitivity says $C inter A=C inter B=0$, and the ambient discriminant is 3-elementary precisely when $3 C^perp subset C$. More generally use an odd prime $p$ below.

The projection $C^perp -> A$ is onto: the character on the gluing subgroup in $B$ prescribed by any $a in A$ extends to $B$, and non-degeneracy represents it by pairing with an element of $B$. The same holds for the other projection. The elementary condition then forces $p A$ and $p B$ into the gluing subgroups and identifies them. This argument does not assume an exponent bound on $B$: if $A$ has exponent at most $p^2$, the resulting isomorphism $p A tilde.equiv p B$ proves that $B$ has exponent at most $p^2$ as well.

On $p A$ define a separate quadratic form $tilde(Q)_(A)(p x)=(p^2 slash 2)q_(A)(x)$ modulo $p$. It is well defined since changing $x$ by an element of $A[p]$ changes this value by a multiple of $p$. It is non-degenerate on the $p^2$ constituents. This is not the restriction of $q_A$, and the original $q_A$ need not descend to $A slash p A$. Define $tilde(Q)_B$ in the same way.

The restriction $phi_0:p A -> p B$ of the gluing is an anti-isometry for these new forms. To see this, choose $(a,b) in C^perp$ for each $a$; then $p b=phi_(0)(p a)$, and orthogonality to $(p x,phi_(0)(p x)) in C$, multiplied by $p$, gives the anti-isometry identity on the polar forms. Since $p$ is odd this also determines the quadratic forms. Put $I="graph"(phi_0)$. The correct reduction is $E=I^perp slash I$. It is $p$-elementary: orthogonality to $I$ and non-degeneracy force $p b=phi_(0)(p a)$ for every $(a,b) in I^perp$.

Further gluings correspond to isotropic subspaces $J=C slash I$ of $E$. Primitivity is preserved by requiring $J$ to meet each of the images of $A[p]$ and $B[p]$ trivially. These images inject into $E$ but may intersect each other.

*Two constituents.* In rank 20 with ambient discriminant $3^6$, two surviving order-9 constituents require $A_8^2$ in the original root lattice. If $A=(ZZ slash 9)^2 perp FF_3^w$, its rank $n$ is at least $16+2w$: extra 3-elementary constituents cost at least two root dimensions each, and saturation preserving the two order-9 constituents cannot increase $w$. Put $N=20-n$ and $B=(ZZ slash 9)^2 perp FF_3^b$. If $h=dim J$, orders give
$ 6=(4+w)+(4+b)-2(2+h), quad b=2-w+2h. $
The minimal number $2+b$ of generators of $B$ is at most $N<=4-2w$. Therefore $w+2h<=0$, forcing $w=h=0$, $b=2$, $N=4$. No nonzero $J$ is possible. For $A_8$, $q(x)=8 slash 9$, so $tilde(Q)_(A)(3x)=1$ modulo 3. The two constituents give the anisotropic binary form $x_1^2+x_2^2$ over $FF_3$. Its orthogonal group has order $2(3+1)=8$, giving the eight anti-isometry graphs when the opposite form exists. Thus the extra branches excluded by the implementation cannot occur in this application.

*One constituent.* Write $A=chevron.l v chevron.r perp W$, $B=chevron.l y chevron.r perp V$ with $q(v)=2a slash 9$ and $q(y)=-2a slash 9$, and put $w=dim W$, $b=dim V$. Let $"Anti"(W,V;h)$ count anti-isometry graphs from $h$-dimensional subspaces of $W$ into $V$, including degenerate subspaces.

If $C$ has no element of order 9, the graph on $3A$ has two choices. For either one, $E$ is a hyperbolic plane $chevron.l e,f chevron.r$ plus $W perp V$, with $e=[(3v,0)]$, $f=[(v,plus.minus y)]$. The subspace $J$ has no $f$ component and is the graph of a linear map from an anti-isometry graph in $W perp V$ to $chevron.l e chevron.r$. For $h=dim(C slash I)$ there are $3^h$ such maps. This branch has multiplicity $2 dot 3^h "Anti"(W,V;h)$ and $|C|=3^(1+h)$, whence $b=s-2-w+2h$.

If $C$ has an order-9 element, let $G$ count all pairs $(v',y')$ of such elements with $q(v')+q(y')=0$. Their cyclic graphs $C_1$ have six generators each, giving $G slash 6$ choices. The non-degenerate cyclic factors split off; their complements are isometric to $W,V$. Now put $h=dim(C slash C_1)$, which is one less than $dim(C slash I)$. The remaining gluing contributes $"Anti"(W,V;h)$. Each final $C tilde.equiv ZZ slash 9 times (ZZ slash 3)^h$ contains $3^h$ cyclic subgroups $C_1$ of order 9, so the multiplicity is $(G slash 6)"Anti"(W,V;h) slash 3^h$. Here $|C|=3^(2+h)$ and $b=s-w+2h$.

In the elementary-form convention $3q$, let $delta_W,delta_V$ be the determinant characters and $phi$ that of the ambient discriminant. Witt reduction gives $delta_V=phi delta_W dot (-1)^(h+1)$ in the first branch and $delta_V=phi delta_W dot (-1)^h$ in the second. These are the two local branches and multiplicities used in #calc("C17"). The coefficients arise from finite graph choices and the two stated overcounts, not from a form on $A slash 3A$. The outer sum over primitive closures and the local mass factors remain the separate operations specified above.
